[size=16pt][b]An assembly Bitcoin node vs Bitcoin Core v31.1: a full mainnet IBD, measured[/b][/size]

2026-09-11. Benchmark tag [tt]bench-2026-09-10[/tt], node commit [tt]4a4872cf[/tt].

[quote][b]CORRECTED 2026-09-13, after a fresh unhandicapped Core baseline.[/b] The
original version of this report was wrong in two directions at once, and the
corrections do not cancel out.

[b]1. The UTXO set was always correct.[/b] This report said the node built a wrong
UTXO set. It did not. The store was later walked offline and quiesced and matched
Core exactly at height 966,496 -- muhash [tt]df1b0340...073d0165[/tt], txouts
165,200,444, bogosize 12,941,799,750, total_amount 2,008,257,300,621,623 sat. The
original failure came from hashing through the live node while its UTXO engine was
still applying: a torn read, not a defect.

[b]2. The "hour ahead of Core" was an artifact, and it is withdrawn.[/b] Core's
21h 11m came from a run that lost 138 minutes between heights 835,000 and 840,000
to a single stalling peer, and that ran under [tt]ionice -c3[/tt] -- the idle I/O
class -- while this node ran with no ionice at all. [b]The stall does not
reproduce.[/b] A fresh Core baseline on 2026-09-12/13, unhandicapped, crossed that
same segment in 16 minutes and finished in [b]19h 14m[/b]. Every run measured on
this box crosses it in 11 to 16 minutes. The margin was one bad peer in one run.

[b]3. The "8 to 12 percent slower" finding is also withdrawn, in our favour.[/b]
Against the unhandicapped baseline, run 23 is FASTER in every segment measured.
See the four-way table below, which replaces the two-way one.[/quote]

This is a report on one fresh mainnet initial block download by an independent
Bitcoin full node written in x86-64 assembly, measured against Bitcoin Core
v31.1 on the same machine -- and, as it turned out, on three defects in the
measurement rather than in the node.

[b]The short version: it built a chain identical to Core's block for block, and a
UTXO set identical to Core's coin for coin.[/b] The capstone that said otherwise
was reading a set that was still moving.

[hr]
[size=12pt][b]The machine, and both configurations[/b][/size]

[table]
[tr][td][b][/b][/td][td][b][/b][/td][/tr]
[tr][td]CPU[/td][td]AMD Ryzen 9 9950X3D, 16 cores / 32 threads[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]RAM[/td][td]123 GB[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]disk[/td][td]Samsung Portable SSD T5, 1.8 TB, ext4, sequential write 0.40 GiB/s[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]kernel[/td][td]Linux 7.0.0-31-generic[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]link[/td][td]2500 Mb/s, no traffic shaping ([tt]fq_codel[/tt] default)[/td][/tr]
[/table]

Both nodes: [tt]dbcache=8192[/tt], fresh datadir, no [tt]assumeutxo[/tt], no pruning, mainnet,
peers from the public network by DNS seed.

[b]On peer counts, which is where IBD comparisons usually go wrong.[/b] Core's
block-download concurrency is not configurable. [tt]MAX_OUTBOUND_FULL_RELAY_CONNECTIONS[/tt]
pins it at 8 outbound full-relay peers regardless of what [tt]maxconnections[/tt] says,
and it keeps up to 16 blocks in flight per peer inside a 1,024-block window.
Earlier comparisons here ran the assembly node at 16 to 64 downloading peers
against Core's 8, which is not a comparison of anything. This run is the first
at [b]8 on both sides[/b].

[hr]
